<h2 id="upgrade-path">Upgrade Path and Risk Controls</h2>

<p class="lh">A dependable Moodle 5.2 rollout follows a staged playbook with explicit readiness gates. The objective is to protect learner continuity while modernizing the platform foundation. Teams should avoid direct production upgrades without full regression evidence.</p>

<ol class="blog-article-list blog-article-list--ordered">
    <li>Clone production to a staging environment with anonymized data where required.</li>
    <li>Run automated and manual regression tests on enrollments, quizzes, certificates, and payments (if applicable).</li>
    <li>Validate backup/restore procedures and define rollback criteria.</li>
    <li>Communicate maintenance windows to learners and support desks.</li>
    <li>Monitor queues, cron tasks, and error logs for 48–72 hours post-launch.</li>
</ol>

<p class="lh">Plugin compatibility remains the most common source of delay. Maintain a plugin registry with owners, compatibility status, replacement plans, and retirement deadlines. If an extension blocks upgrade readiness and has no viable support path, escalate decommission decisions early rather than carrying outdated components into production.</p>

<p class="lh">Testing depth should be proportional to business criticality. Regulated organizations should include evidence capture for audit and compliance review, while global enterprises should validate localization behavior, timezone handling, and role-specific permission controls across representative cohorts.</p>

